1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
This is how most demurrage claims
still get calculated.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
I've spent over ten years in shipping
and chartering operations.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,500
Every laytime statement I've seen has been
rebuilt by hand, from a scanned SOF.

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,000
One wrong stoppage time and the
demurrage figure is wrong.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,500
So I built the tool I wanted.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Stoppages get recorded as they happen —
not reconstructed from a PDF weeks later.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,500
One tap for the start time.
One tap to close it.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The laytime calculation runs
off that record.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Allowed time, time used, what's excluded
and why — and the exposure figure.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Calculated the same way every single time.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And every interval is on the record.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Which hours counted, which were excluded,
which stoppage caused it.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
When a counterparty disputes the claim,
the working is already there.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,500
It's called Voyantix. It's early.

15
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:17,000
I'm building it around how this work
actually gets done.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,000
If you handle laytime or demurrage and you'd
tell me what I've got wrong — I'd like to hear it.
